cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/invariants-dev && cat > docs/INVARIANTS.md <<'EOF'
# Engineering invariants: execution authority, side effects, failure semantics, change cost

Status: in force on `v1/invariants-impl` (not yet released). This file is the map from each rule to the code that enforces it, the test that proves it, and why it exists. It is checked by `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, so a row cannot point at a file that moved, and a rule named in code or tests cannot be missing here. It replaces no other document: `docs/EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE.md` describes the spine, `docs/v1.2.0/SEMANTIC_AND_ARCHITECTURAL_INTEGRITY.md` the acceptance gates.

How to read a row: **Enforced in** is the authoritative boundary (where the rule is decided, not where it is described). **Proven by** is a test that fails if the rule is removed. **Why** is the incident or audit finding, so a later change can tell a deliberate rule from an accident.

## Rules

| ID | Invariant | Enforced in | Proven by | Why (provenance) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| I-LIFE-1 | A lifecycle change is decided at the authoritative boundary, under the driver lock: `run` never overwrites an existing operation id; `resume` and `decide` re-read the record after the lock is held; a COMPLETE operation or unit never leaves COMPLETE; unknown statuses are rejected at load. | `cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts`, `cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts`, `cli/src/core/operation_runner_persistence.ts` | `cli/test/operation_entry_integrity.test.ts`, `cli/test/operation_schema.test.ts` | Audit 2026-11-17: `operation_cli.ts` loaded state before the lock was taken, so a stale snapshot could be written over a finished record; `run` with an existing id replaced its history. |
| I-EFFECT-1 | A mutating MCP `tools/call` over HTTP is delivered at most once per model request. Only a definitive "no such route" answer (404/405/501 from `/message`) may fall back to the legacy URL; any other failure is reported as unknown. | `cli/src/mcp.ts` | `cli/test/mcp_tool_call_delivery.test.ts` | Audit 2026-11-17: `sendRequest` re-POSTed the same payload after any fetch failure, including a reset or timeout after the server had received it. |
| I-EFFECT-2 | An unknown outcome (timeout, cancel, lost connection) is never reported as a confirmed failure. The receipt says OUTCOME UNKNOWN, and a mutating tool in that state counts as possibly mutated, so the unverified-change obligation is raised. | `cli/src/core/spine.ts`, `cli/src/tools.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts` | `cli/test/spine_unknown_outcome.test.ts` | Audit: a timed-out `write_file` produced `candidateMutated=false`, so the workspace could change with no verification owed. |
| I-EFFECT-3 | A call identical to one whose outcome was left unknown by a crash is not re-issued silently on resume, even in auto mode: it needs an explicit approval. The identity is a hash of the tool and canonical arguments written ahead of the call. | `cli/src/core/operation_identity.ts`, `cli/src/core/state.ts`, `cli/src/agent.ts` | `cli/test/resume_reissue_interrupted.test.ts`, `cli/test/fault_kill_mid_tool_call.test.ts` | Audit: recovery marked the operation interrupted but the model could re-issue the same effect on resume. |
| I-EFFECT-4 | Dry-run never executes a mutating capability, in whichever layer would simulate it. The bridge simulates file edits and the shell; the spine simulates every other mutating capability (extension tools, the knowledge store). | `cli/src/core/spine.ts` | `cli/test/dry_run_effects.test.ts` | Audit: extension tools and `manage_knowledge` ran for real under `--dry-run` while their receipt said `simulated: true`. |
| I-AUTH-1 | A repository cannot weaken verification-path protection. `.nexus-security.json` may set `mode` off or record, or `allow` paths, only in a workspace the operator has trusted; approve, block and `extra` only add protection and are always honoured. | `cli/src/security.ts` | `cli/test/verification_guard.test.ts` | Audit: cloning a repository that shipped `"mode": "off"` switched the guard off for the agent working in it. |
| I-AUTH-2 | Every tool offered to the model has a capability with an explicit effect class. MCP and plugin tools are registered after connection as `extension` capabilities (conservative: mutating, `egress` for a remote server, `exec` for a local process or plugin), so the spine can run them and review mode asks first. | `cli/src/core/extension_capabilities.ts`, `cli/src/agent.ts` | `cli/test/mcp_tool_through_spine.test.ts` | Audit: every MCP tool call failed with `FAIL-001 not registered`; reproduced at runtime before the fix. |
| I-AUTH-3 | The base URL an operator gives a provider follows the same rules as a provider file's: no credentials in the URL, no fragment, no cleartext http to a non-local host. The provider key is sent to that URL. | `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/provider_safety.ts` | `cli/test/provider_safety.test.ts` | Audit: the override accepted `http://public-host/` and sent the API key in cleartext. |
| I-ACCEPT-1 | The validation receipt written at completion binds the verifier's actual output (its digest), not a constant, and a receipt that could not be persisted is not reported as a verified completion. | `cli/src/agent.ts` | `cli/test/completion_receipt_binding.test.ts` | Audit: `stdoutHash` was the hash of a fixed string, so two different runs produced identical receipts. |
| I-ACCEPT-2 | A reviewer's ACCEPT, an engineering assessment or any model text never overrides a deterministic gate: a failing or absent verifier, a scope violation, or an unproven criterion forces REJECT. The reviewer's ACCEPT is evidence, not acceptance authority. | `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts` | `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`, `cli/test/ceri_semantic_integrity.test.ts` | Existing control (credited, not new): `parseIndependentReviewVerdict` fails closed. Pinned here so the review contract cannot weaken it. |
| I-PROVIDER-1 | A subagent sizes its requests from its own connection's advertised limits, discovered once, not from the 64k fallback. | `cli/src/agent.ts` | `cli/test/subagent_capability_discovery.test.ts` | Audit: `runSubagent` never called capability discovery; a helper on a 24k model was admitted against 65536. |
| I-PROVIDER-2 | A chat answer ending with `finish_reason=content_filter` is not a complete answer. It is refused, not retried and not failed over. | `cli/src/client.ts` | `cli/test/client_robustness.test.ts` | Audit: a filtered answer was returned as the model's reply. |
| I-CHANGE-1 | The implementer prompt states the change-scope rules: smallest change, no abstraction nothing needs, a refactor keeps behaviour, find the reason before removing code, keep comments, tests and docs true, state side effects and unchecked assumptions. Instructions only: acceptance still rests on the verifier and the review. | `cli/src/agent.ts` | `cli/test/change_scope_prompt.test.ts` | Follow-up assignment 2026-11-17: nothing told the implementer about scope or rationale. The text is short on purpose: it is sent with every request and context-budget tests are calibrated against the prompt size. |
| I-CHANGE-2 | The review verdict carries an engineering assessment (purpose, evidence, boundaryImpact, invariantImpact, failureBehaviour, maintainabilityImpact, validation, residualRisk). It is required, complete, for a significant change (five or more files, 300 or more changed lines, or any file under a core directory) and optional otherwise. A `change-risk:` defect (hidden side effect, unneeded abstraction, behaviour change inside a refactor, lost rationale) blocks ACCEPT. | `cli/src/core/independent_review.ts` | `cli/test/independent_review.test.ts`, `cli/test/ceri_semantic_integrity.test.ts` | Follow-up assignment 2026-11-17. Extends the existing verdict schema; adds no reviewer and no gate beyond the existing fail-closed parse. |

## Where rationale lives (no append-only history)

- The rule and its reason: the row above.
- Why a specific line exists: the comment or test header at the enforcing code, which names the rule id.
- Scenarios that exercise the rules with expected outcomes frozen outside the code: `cli/test/fixtures/ceri/semantic_integrity.scenarios.json` (category `change_cost` and the existing ones). These run real gating code with scripted inputs; they do not measure whether a live model produces the finding.
- Roadmap and open work: `docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md`.

## Credited as already enforced (verified in this pass, not changed)

Reviewer cannot self-accept and a frozen, invariant-checked `TurnResult`; upgrade-journal recovery; the operation driver lock with merge reconciliation; per-endpoint credential and egress binding on failover; atomic task-state writes with quarantine of corrupt files; EXECUTION_UNKNOWN blocking replay of an operation unit; failover wait treats any non-5xx/429/408 answer as "the server is serving" (documented in `LocalLlmClient.probeAvailability`; a permanent auth failure must surface, not be waited out).

## Known gaps (recorded, not fixed here)

| Gap | Why it stays open | Next step |
|---|---|---|
| Intent-record write failure is fail-open: the call proceeds when the write-ahead record cannot be written (pinned by `cli/test/durable_continuity.test.ts`). | Changing it fails closed on a full disk and needs an owner decision. | Decide the policy; flip with the test. |
